Lesson 026: High-NA EUV improves resolution by raising numerical aperture, but its value depends on whether it removes more manufacturing complexity than it introduces.

Lesson 027: EUV pellicles protect reusable reticles from repeating contamination defects, while transmission and thermal behavior can affect scanner throughput.

Lesson 028: An EUV mask is a patterned multilayer mirror, so reflectivity, absorber geometry and buried defects all influence what is actually printed on the wafer.

Lesson 029: Mask inspection and repair separate harmless imperfections from printable defects before one master-pattern error can repeat across many exposures.

Lesson 030: Multi-beam e-beam writers use massive parallelism to manufacture increasingly complex mask shapes without letting write time scale directly with pattern complexity.

The big idea

A chip designer does **not** simply draw the transistor or wire shape they want on the wafer and send that same shape to the mask shop.

At leading-edge dimensions, light diffraction, scanner optics, photoresist chemistry, mask effects and later process steps distort what finally prints.

So the mask is deliberately made **wrong in order for the wafer to come out right**.

That transformation is computational lithography.

A simplified flow is:

desired wafer layout → lithography model → OPC or ILT correction → mask manufacturability checks → mask data preparation → mask writer

This is the software layer sitting between chip design and the physical reticle from the last several lessons.

## 1. Why the original layout cannot simply be printed

Imagine wanting a perfectly square black shape on a wall, but your projector blurs every sharp corner and spreads light into neighboring regions.

If you project the square exactly as drawn, the wall image will not be square.

The semiconductor version is much harder because features can be close to the practical resolution limit of the scanner.

Nearby lines interact through diffraction and interference. Corners round. Line ends shorten. Dense patterns and isolated patterns print differently. Dose and focus variations change dimensions across the process window.

The mask therefore has to anticipate those distortions.

The goal is not:

**make the mask match the chip layout.**

The goal is:

**make the wafer image match the chip layout after the entire imaging process acts on it.**

## 2. OPC: deliberately distort the mask

**Optical proximity correction, or OPC,** starts with a model of how the scanner, mask, resist and process will print a pattern.

Software simulates the wafer image, compares it with the intended target, modifies the mask, and repeats.

Typical corrections can include:

- moving an edge slightly outward or inward;
- extending a line end;
- reshaping a corner;
- changing local widths;
- adding tiny assist features near the real circuit feature.

Those assist features may never be intended to print as working circuit structures. Their job is to alter the optical field so the **main** feature prints more accurately.

This is why a modern reticle can look surprisingly different from the clean geometric layout a circuit designer originally drew.

## 3. SRAFs: features that help without becoming features

A common example is the **sub-resolution assist feature**, or SRAF.

Suppose one isolated line prints differently from a line surrounded by neighbors.

Software can place small shapes near the isolated line so, optically, it behaves more like a dense pattern.

The SRAF is designed to influence diffraction but remain below the threshold required to print as a real wafer feature.

That creates a narrow operating window:

